The podium's national anthems (anthem-data.js, built by tools/anthems/build.js
from the files kept in tools/anthems/). Only the notes are taken: an excerpt
of each melody, with a bass line, as a list of pitches and lengths that the
game's own synthesizer plays (podium-sound.js). No recording is used. Every
composition is in the public domain (each composer died more than 70 years
ago, or is unknown and centuries old; Thailand's was also published as a
state announcement in the Royal Gazette). Each source and its licence:

- Hymne Monégasque (Monaco; Théophile Bellando de Castro (1841), arranged by Charles Albrecht (1867)): https://nationalanthems.info/mc~.jpg by nationalanthems.info (score set by Jo Hyo-roe), CC BY 4.0, read by hand from its score.
- God Save the King (United Kingdom; Anonymous (18th century)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Royal_Anthem.mid by Rabanus Flavus (Peter Gerloff), CC BY 4.0, read mechanically from its MIDI file.
- Wilhelmus (Netherlands; Anonymous (16th century; Valerius, 1626)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelmus_bladmuziek.jpg by Wilhelmus (Wikimedia Commons), Public domain, read by hand from its score.
- Advance Australia Fair (Australia; Peter Dodds McCormick (1878)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Advance_Australia_Fair.mid by Brian Yap, from the 1907 first edition, Public domain, read mechanically from its MIDI file.
- Marcha Real (Spain; Anonymous (Marcha Granadera, 1761)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcha_Real_de_Espa%C3%B1a.pdf by Alberto Betancourt (piano transcription), Public domain, read by hand from its score.
- O Canada (Canada; Calixa Lavallée (1880)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:O_Canada.mid by Denelson83, Public domain, read mechanically from its MIDI file.
- La Marseillaise (France; Claude Joseph Rouget de Lisle (1792)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allons,_enfants_de_la_patrie.mid by Rabanus Flavus (Peter Gerloff), CC0 1.0, read mechanically from its MIDI file.
- Phleng Chat Thai (Thailand; Phra Chenduriyang (1932)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phleng_Chat_Thai_Sheet_Music_(official).jpg by Royal Thai Government Gazette, 1939, Public domain, read by hand from its score.
- Das Lied der Deutschen (Germany; Joseph Haydn (1797)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einigkeit_und_Recht.mid by Rabanus Flavus (Peter Gerloff), CC0 1.0, read mechanically from its MIDI file.
- Kimigayo (Japan; Hayashi Hiromori and Oku Yoshiisa (1880)): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kimigayo_MIDI.mid by www.nationalanthems.info, CC BY 4.0, read mechanically from its MIDI file.
- God Defend New Zealand (New Zealand; John Joseph Woods (1876)): https://nationalanthems.info/nz~.jpg by nationalanthems.info (the New Zealand Gazette's setting), CC BY 4.0, read by hand from its score.

CC0 1.0: https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
CC BY 4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ (the works so marked
are credited above and were adapted: reduced to one melody line and one bass
line, cut to an excerpt, and where the score gave no bass a plain one was
written for this game, as each file in tools/anthems/transcribed/ says).
The MIDI files are kept unchanged in tools/anthems/sources/, and the scores
that were read by hand in tools/anthems/scores/.
There is no anthem for Italy or Brazil: no score of either free to use here
was found, and the game's own fanfare (an original tune) plays instead.
